<article class="env-definition" id="0EQE">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0EQE">Definition <span data-tag="0EQE">82.17.1</span>.</a> In Situation <a href="/tag/0EDT" data-tag="0EDT">82.2.1</a> let $X/B$ be good. Assume $X$ is integral and $n = \dim _\delta (X)$. Let $\mathcal{L}$ be an invertible $\mathcal{O}_ X$-module. </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
For any nonzero meromorphic section $s$ of $\mathcal{L}$ we define the <em>Weil divisor associated to $s$</em> is the $(n - 1)$-cycle 
</p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  \text{div}_\mathcal {L}(s) = \sum \text{ord}_{Z, \mathcal{L}}(s) [Z]  \]
</div>
<p>
 defined in Spaces over Fields, Definition <a href="/tag/0EPU" data-tag="0EPU">72.7.4</a>. This makes sense because Weil divisors have $\delta $-dimension $n - 1$ by Lemma <a href="/tag/0EPZ" data-tag="0EPZ">82.12.1</a>. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
We define <em>Weil divisor associated to $\mathcal{L}$</em> as 
</p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  c_1(\mathcal{L}) \cap [X] = \text{class of }\text{div}_\mathcal {L}(s) \in \mathop{\mathrm{CH}}\nolimits _{n - 1}(X)  \]
</div>
<p>
 where $s$ is any nonzero meromorphic section of $\mathcal{L}$ over $X$. This is well defined by Spaces over Fields, Lemma <a href="/tag/0EPT" data-tag="0EPT">72.7.3</a>. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
</article>